                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[b]pyromind[/b] is a blast to play in every sense of the word. you're controlling a mind that has to avoid or defuse bombs appearing after every turn. in classic mode nothing happens if you don't move, so technically you can take your time and think of the next step, but sooner or later you'll be caught in a chain explosion anyway and mind blown! I'd really like an optional chain explosion highlighter, but it's not a game to complete, it's more about reaching a high score.\n\nregular bombs have a 10-turn counter and explode in a 3x3 area, green ones go off in a straight line in all 4 directions, triggering whatever's in their way, of course, and you don't want to wait until an empress reaches 0. as you progress further and further in a run, different things start to happen, like 2 bombs appearing every turn, and don't forget that you can leave the screen on one side to appear on the other, it can make a huge difference.\n\ntime attack is the same, except you only have 60 seconds to score as much as possible, and in the battle arena you are up against your friends or various bots. getting a 30-point advantage here starts moving the laser in the middle towards the opponent, and you can snipe them by letting green bombs explode instead of defusing them, but it's still a really tough challenge, though I got a lot further than I'd expected, managed to complete 7 matches, while I only reached tier 5 in classic mode and tier 4 in time attack.\n\nthere are various minds (characters) to unlock with their own special skills, like more points for green bombs, more points for defusing bombs that are about to explode or about to appear, and so on. you get these new minds by completing certain tiers in the game, and some you have to buy with diamonds, but it's really (and I mean EXTREMELY!) grindy.\n\na single diamond is awarded for 100 points or for defusing an empress bomb, so during an average run you can get maybe 5, a bit more if you're lucky. you can also unlock a minigame where clicking on really fast things for a diamond can get you more, but it's really not worth bothering with, and the most expensive unlock costs 5000 (five thousand!) diamonds, the one before 3000, the one before 1000, so if you want all of them, you're in for an extremely long haul. either more diamonds should be awarded (for winning arena matches or even just rounds, or 1 for 100 points, 2 for 200, etc.) or costs should be lowered.\n\nfrom a technical standpoint it's mostly fine, there are resolution options and windowed mode, rebindable controls too, but the exit button is easy to miss in the menu, have to press esc instead, and audio settings are lacking as well. no volume sliders, and there's only sound & random music, sound & a specific track (there are only 3), only sound or everything off.\n\nanother thing I'd like changed is how fast restart works by pressing tab, as you still have to stare at the screen for a few seconds while the game tells you that you're still on the same tier you were during the previous 500 attempts. sure, it's faster than waiting for all the points to be counted slowly, but it should be instantaneous. and leaderboards should either have their own in-game screen or bring up the steam overlay and show them there, not in a new window in my browser.\n\nall in all, it's a really enjoyable game, but keep in mind that it's extremely difficult, frustrating and random, unlocks will take forever, as will achievements, if you're into them, but the price is more than reasonable, so it's worth considering even for a couple hours of fun.",
